From 18e928ad3fc1be44ad016c7b0ac5120f47ca4090 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christian Breunig <christian@breunig.cc>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 22:58:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] ippool: reload the [ip-pool] section on configuration
 reload

The pools were built once in DEFINE_INIT2 and there was no
EV_CONFIG_RELOAD handler, so any change to [ip-pool] needed a daemon
restart - which drops every established session. This is the single most
common reason to restart rather than reload.

The difficulty is that a leased address had no representation in the pool
at all: get_ip() unlinks the item from pool->items and hands it to the
session, and only put_ip() puts it back. So the pool could not tell which
of its addresses were in use, and rebuilding it would have handed out
addresses that were already assigned.

Track leases in a per-pool "used" list - two lines in get_ip()/put_ip() -
and reconcile against it:

  - Rebuild the pools from scratch rather than computing an incremental
    delta. The generate callbacks pair gateway with peer addresses and
    group net30 blocks over a whole pool at a time, so re-running them
    exactly as they run at startup is the only way to be sure the result
    matches the configuration.
  - Hand the resulting addresses to the pool object that already exists
    under the same name, rather than replacing the object. Addresses
    leased out point back at it and outlive the reload.
  - Take a snapshot of every address leased anywhere, and drop those from
    the newly built pools. This has to be global rather than per pool:
    the configuration may move an address between pools or rename a pool,
    and in both cases a session is still using the address even though the
    pool it came from no longer claims it.
  - A leased address the pool no longer offers is marked stale; put_ip()
    frees it on release instead of returning it to the free list. Sessions
    are never disturbed - shrinking a pool just stops the address being
    handed out again once its session ends.
  - A pool dropped from the configuration is retired: unlinked from
    pool_list so it can no longer be selected, free addresses released,
    outstanding leases marked stale. The object is kept until its last
    lease returns, then reaped at the next reload.

The address sets involved are large - a /16 pool against a few thousand
leases - so the lookups are binary searches over sorted arrays rather than
list walks. A 65280-address pool with 5000 leases reconciles in a few ms.

Adding an [ip-pool] section to a configuration that started without one
still requires a restart: the handler is only registered when the section
was present at startup.

Two pre-existing leaks had to be fixed, since a reload turns both from a
one-off into per-reload growth:

  - find_pool() ignored the name the caller had allocated when the pool
    already existed. On reload every range option looks its pool up again.
  - The gw_list/tunnel_list staging lists are routinely left non-empty by
    the generators - generate_pool_p2p() never touches gw_list when
    gw-ip-address is set - and were never drained.

Also fixed session_restore() (USE_BACKUP only, not built by default) to put
a restored item on the used list. Without it put_ip() would list_del() an
entry that was never linked.
---
 accel-pppd/extra/ippool.c | 539 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 531 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/accel-pppd/extra/ippool.c b/accel-pppd/extra/ippool.c
index d790df8..3a6101b 100644
--- a/accel-pppd/extra/ippool.c
+++ b/accel-pppd/extra/ippool.c
@@ -31,17 +31,29 @@ struct ippool_t
 	struct list_head gw_list;
 	struct list_head tunnel_list;
 	struct list_head items;
+	/*
+	 * Addresses handed out by get_ip() and not yet returned. Without this
+	 * a leased address has no representation in the pool at all, and a
+	 * reload has no way to tell which addresses are still in use.
+	 */
+	struct list_head used;
